      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt In a special episode of “The Poynter Report Podcast,” the CNN political correspondent discusses Democrats’ sweeping victories and what comes next Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION CNN&rsquo;s John King has seen a lot of elections. This one was different. CNN&rsquo;s John King, shown here in 2021. (AP Photo/Ross D. Franklin) On Tuesday night, CNN&rsquo;s John King stood in front of his Magic Wall at the network&rsquo;s Washington, D.C., bureau. King worked his magic, calling up numbers from across the country. The Democrats were rolling up big wins in Ohio, North Carolina, Michigan, Maine, Kansas and, believe it or not, Texas. A blue ripple turned into a blue wave. A blue wave turned into a blue tsunami. And a blue tsunami turned into whatever Tuesday was &mdash; a hugely triumphant night for Democrats. And what appeared to be a complete repudiation of President Donald Trump and the MAGA movement. What the heck happened? King joined me on the latest episode of &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; to talk about the midterms, and I asked him right out of the gate: When he woke up last Tuesday morning, did he expect such a dominant night for the Democrats? &ldquo;Not at the size or the scope,&rdquo; King told me. While King is known for his work in front of the Magic Wall on election night, he is most proud of his work out on the road &mdash; sitting at kitchen tables with voters and hearing firsthand what they think. King told me, &ldquo;We&rsquo;ve been on the road constantly. We went to 18 states. We did more than 30 trips. We went to the places that we knew would help answer that question: How big would it be? A big night for the Democrats, and if big, how big?&rdquo; And how big was it? &ldquo;This is historic,&rdquo; King told me. In this episode of &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast,&rdquo; King and I dig into how James Talarico won in Texas, the controversial Vivek Ramaswamy ad that might have cost him the Ohio governor&rsquo;s race, why North Carolina turned blue, the close races in Florida and the hotly contested race in Michigan. And much more, including what it all means going forward. In addition to watching on YouTube , you can also find the show on Apple , Spotify and most places where you find podcasts. &lsquo;60 Minutes&rsquo; interviews Trump President Donald Trump, left, being interviewed by &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; correspondent Ross Douthat. (Courtesy of CBS News) &ldquo;60 Minutes,&rdquo; the iconic CBS News show at a crossroads under new leadership, made a big splash Sunday, landing the first extensive sit-down interview with President Donald Trump since Tuesday&rsquo;s midterm elections. New &ldquo;60 Minutes&rdquo; correspondent Ross Douthat handled the interview, which stretched across two segments. ( Here&rsquo;s the entire extended interview , including parts that did not air Sunday.) The two discussed a wide range of topics, but the early focus in the first segment was the Republicans&rsquo; disastrous performance in the midterms. Trump said, &ldquo;Well, No. 1, I was not on the ballot. But I campaigned hard for some people. I think if I was on the ballot, it would&rsquo;ve been a much different situation because you saw the level of popularity and love. There was love. We had massive rallies. You had to transfer those voters, however, to candidates that they didn&rsquo;t like. But there&rsquo;s something beyond that. They didn&rsquo;t like the war. They didn&rsquo;t like the fact that gasoline went up, because I had it down at a low, and there&rsquo;s &mdash; a great dislike, incredibly, for Israel.&rdquo; Trump went through many of the candidates one by one, and then was asked about Vice President JD Vance&rsquo;s comments that the war with Iran cost the GOP in the midterms. Trump said, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t disagree.&rdquo; During the second segment, Douthat asked about Trump&rsquo;s net worth rising by approximately $3 billion during his second term and pressed him on possible corruption. Trump claimed ignorance, saying, &ldquo;I devote almost no time to my net worth. I don&rsquo;t care.&rdquo; But he did claim that his net worth did not go up by that much. To his credit, Douthat kept pressing him. At one point, Douthat astutely asked, &ldquo;Do you think the people &mdash; the people who are investing in the company that you co-own with your sons, who are coming from foreign countries who want things from your government &mdash; don&rsquo;t you think they think that they are buying influence?&rdquo; Trump said, &ldquo;I don&rsquo;t know,&rdquo; before going on a rambling answer that didn&rsquo;t really answer the question. Here&rsquo;s a transcript of what aired on Sunday, which also included Trump&rsquo;s plans to work with Democrats, who might run for president in 2028, the political futures of Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth and Secretary of State Marco Rubio, the Russia-Ukraine war, immigration, artificial intelligence and UFOs. The interview ended with Douthat asking Trump about what will happen to him after he dies. Trump said, &ldquo;Well, I&rsquo;m a religious person, so in theory I go to heaven. &hellip; I hope so. I don&rsquo;t want to go to hell.&rdquo; Douthat later asked, &ldquo;Let&rsquo;s say you pass away, you&rsquo;re entering the next life, and all you have to do to enter paradise is feel love and forgiveness for James Comey, Nancy Pelosi, anyone who you have expressed hate for over the years. Do you think you can do it?&rdquo; Trump said, &ldquo;Well, then I wouldn&rsquo;t be real, would I? I mean, if I said to you, sure, the easy answer to that is &lsquo;Oh, yes, I could forgive.&rsquo;&rdquo; Trump added, &ldquo;I found them to be extremely dishonest people, very bad people, very bad for our country. I love our country far more than I can love people and people like that.&rdquo; He concluded by saying, &ldquo;I probably cannot do that, no, because I do hate some of my enemies and I always will.&rdquo; Douthat did a solid job, asking good, tough and fair (and a couple of offbeat) questions, and jumping in when necessary to keep Trump from rambling and going off focus. The result was a revealing interview, more illuminating than most interviews with Trump. Getting into the ring In a story that you had to check around to make sure was actually true, President Donald Trump announced Saturday that he had offered the White House press secretary job to Dana White, the CEO of the Ultimate Fighting Championship. Trump announced it on Truth Social , writing, &ldquo;I am pleased to announce that the Great Dana White, a violent Fighter and Warrior, has been offered the job of Press Secretary of the United States. I have requested that he bring with him assistants, even though some are not from our Country, of Islam Makhachev, Khabib Nurmagomedov, Justin Gaethje, Daniel Cormier, Amanda Nunes, and Kayla Harris. We need these people to defend us against the Lunatic Left. Dana will be a Press Secretary like none other. Dana, please take the job!&rdquo; CNN reported on Saturday that White &ldquo;declined the unorthodox idea.&rdquo; However, White said at a UFC event in London on Saturday night that he was flying to Washington to meet with Trump today. White said, &ldquo;We have a meeting on Monday, so I&rsquo;m sure I&rsquo;ll have more to talk about after that.&rdquo; When asked how he could balance being White House press secretary while also running the UFC, White said, &ldquo;Yeah, yeah, I got a lot of jobs already.&rdquo; This seemed so outlandish that news organizations wondered if Trump was being serious. The New York Times&rsquo; Erica L. Green wrote , &ldquo;It was unclear if Mr. Trump&rsquo;s out-of-the-blue offer to Mr. White was meant to be a joke or was a serious offer aimed at projecting defiance after his midterm election losses.&rdquo; CNN&rsquo;s Kaitlan Collins and Isabelle D&rsquo;Antonio wrote that &ldquo;one aide said Trump was joking about the offer.&rdquo; Then again, Trump does need a press secretary. Karoline Leavitt, who had served as press secretary since the start of Trump&rsquo;s second term, stepped down in August following the birth of her second child. She was replaced by Katie Zacharia, who held just one disastrous press conference and was forced out of the job after a mere five days &mdash; the shortest stint in the history of the job. Collins and D&rsquo;Antonio wrote, &ldquo;Trump has struggled to find someone to fill a crucial position that he is personally invested in &mdash; a matter that has only become more important after Democrats won control of Congress and pledged to investigate his administration.&rdquo; Still, Dana White? On Sunday, Trump shared a cartoon video of White as press secretary and UFC fighters beating up reporters after they asked questions. A wild theory Wait, did Trump offer the job to the right Dana White? A conspiracy theory spread on social media over the weekend: Someone in Trump&rsquo;s inner circle suggested he hire Dana White as press secretary. But that person wasn&rsquo;t talking about the UFC CEO. They were talking about someone named Dana W. White, the chief Pentagon spokesperson during Trump&rsquo;s first administration. The Independent&rsquo;s Isabel Keane wrote , &ldquo;In addition to serving as the communications policy lead for the Department of Defense, Dana W. White previously worked for the Heritage Foundation, the far-right think tank. She has also been a columnist for The Wall Street Journal and a commentator on Fox News &mdash; making her seemingly a great fit for the role.&rdquo; So was it all a mix-up? Some on social media compared it to the time Rudy Giuliani held a press conference at Four Seasons Total Landscaping instead of the luxury hotel. Dana W. White responded to all of this, writing on LinkedIn, &ldquo;To everyone texting me congratulations: thank you, but you have the wrong Dana White. I&rsquo;ve stood at the podium and taken questions from the Pentagon press corps. I have never stood in an octagon. I&rsquo;d like to keep it that way. It is nice for once that that Dana White is being confused with this Dana White.&rdquo; Who will run for president?   Vice President JD Vance speaks at a campaign rally in Rome, N.Y., last week. (Tom Brenner/Pool Photo via AP) Final results of the midterm elections are still being tallied, but some media outlets are already looking ahead to who will run for president in 2028. Certainly, the midterms have changed the narrative. For example, Vice President JD Vance, once considered a lock to be the 2028 Republican nominee, might get dragged down by the Trump chaos that was seemingly just rejected by the American voters. Two years is a long time, and much could happen between now and the 2028 election. Vance might not even run. But it&rsquo;s never too early to start looking ahead. The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s Philip Wegmann and Natalie Andrews wrote that Tuesday&rsquo;s &ldquo;brutal midterm drubbing&rdquo; is &ldquo;scrambling Vance&rsquo;s political future.&rdquo; Wegmann and Andrews reported, &ldquo;Some political allies have told the vice president that they no longer believe any Republican could win a presidential election in two years.&rdquo; So if not Vance, then who? The Washington Post&rsquo;s Natalie Allison, Katherine Doyle, Nancy Cook and Isaac Arnsdorf wrote Sunday, &ldquo;Potential Vance challengers smell blood in the water after brutal GOP losses.&rdquo; On the potential challengers, the Post wrote, &ldquo;Tuesday night&rsquo;s GOP bludgeoning has only emboldened some of them. Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis embarked on a media blitz in recent days as he prepares a potential run, according to three people briefed on his plans. Georgia Gov. Brian Kemp is weighing a bid, said a person with knowledge of his plans. And Sen. Ted Cruz of Texas is planning to work with longtime consultant Jeff Roe of Axiom Strategies if he enters the race, said two people with knowledge of his thinking.&rdquo; The Post added, &ldquo;Donors have continued to urge Secretary of State Marco Rubio to jump into the race. But Rubio has said he intends to support Vance if he runs, and has privately signaled little interest in a 2028 bid in recent months.&rdquo; Other possible long shots include Florida Sen. Rick Scott, Louisiana Sen. John Kennedy, Arkansas Gov. Sarah Huckabee Sanders, former Virginia Gov. Glenn Youngkin and maybe even former Vice President Mike Pence. Meanwhile, on the Democratic side, The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s Ken Thomas and John McCormick wrote, &ldquo;The New Stars and Presidential Hopefuls Shaking Up Democrats&rsquo; 2028 Field.&rdquo; Thomas and McCormick broke the candidates into three categories: The Obama/Biden alums (Kamala Harris, Pete Buttigieg, Rahm Emanuel); the governors (Illinois Gov. JB Pritzker, California Gov. Gavin Newsom, Kentucky Gov. Andy Beshear, Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro, Maryland Gov. Wes Moore); and those from The Hill (New York Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, Georgia Sens. Raphael Warnock and Jon Ossoff, Arizona Sens. Mark Kelly and Ruben Gallego, Michigan Sen. Elissa Slotkin, New Jersey Sen. Cory Booker and California Rep. Ro Khanna). DeSantis calls for changes Mediaite&rsquo;s Sean James reported that outgoing Florida Gov. Ron DeSantis gave an interview at the University of Notre Dame over the weekend and called for conservative media to undergo a &ldquo;big change&rdquo; in the wake of the midterm results. DeSantis said, &ldquo;I think you need more bottom-up, I think you need less focus on the presidency, and I think you need more focus on not just people running for elected office, people that are involved in the conservative groups, (but) I think we need a big change in kind of the conservative media culture. Because a lot of that is a grift, and it&rsquo;s pay-to-play, and it&rsquo;s just not offering very much in the way of strong ideas and things that are really going to illuminate your understanding.&rdquo; He added, &ldquo;There&rsquo;s all these different things that are not doing well on the right, and I think we deserve an American right that&rsquo;s fit to fight and is ready to go for &hellip; a decade of battles, just to get to a point where we&rsquo;re able to avert disaster and put us on a solid path.&rdquo; Attiah won&rsquo;t return to the Post Former Washington Post columnist Karen Attiah, shown here during her appearance on &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; last year. In September 2025, The Washington Post fired opinion columnist Karen Attiah, who claimed she was dismissed because of social media posts about gun control and race in the aftermath of the shooting death of far-right activist Charlie Kirk. Not long after her firing, Attiah joined me on an episode of &ldquo;The Poynter Report Podcast&rdquo; to talk about the situation. Attiah challenged her firing, and, in August, an arbitrator ruled the Post wrongfully terminated her and ordered the paper to reinstate her with back pay. But, over the weekend, Attiah announced on her Substack that she will not return. Attiah gave the background of everything that happened. Then she wrote, &ldquo;While I remain proud of the victory, I came to the painful realization that returning to The Washington Post meant coming back without the guaranteed freedom and institutional support to do what I loved: use my voice, seek truth, and serve readers.&rdquo; She added, &ldquo;I have decided not to return to what The Washington Post has become. I have accepted a settlement with the Post and will move forward with my life and career. As excruciating as this battle has been, it was one worth fighting. I am deeply grateful to everyone who supported me throughout this fight and helped me celebrate the victory. I am also indebted to the readers who have continued to believe in my work and who still believe in the power of journalism.&rdquo; Media tidbits Newly elected Texas Sen.-elect James Talarico made a surprise cameo during the cold open on &ldquo;Saturday Night Live&rdquo; and even made the signature announcement of &ldquo;Live from New York, it&rsquo;s Saturday night!&rdquo; Speaking of &ldquo;SNL,&rdquo; here&rsquo;s The Atlantic&rsquo;s Megan Garber with &ldquo;On &lsquo;SNL,&rsquo; the Struggle (For Power) Was Real.&rdquo; Katie Miller announced she is ending her podcast now that her husband, Stephen Miller, has been named Trump&rsquo;s chief of staff. She wrote on X , &ldquo;I make every decision based on what&rsquo;s best for my children and our family. With @StephenM taking on this new role for President Trump, I&rsquo;ve decided to stop recording new podcast episodes. Nothing is more important than raising our four kids. While I&rsquo;ve loved the podcast, the time commitment is too great and now is the time to step away. I&rsquo;ll be cheering President Trump and his team on from outside the administration.&rdquo; &ldquo;Sunday Night Football&rdquo; play-by-play announcer Mike Tirico missed Sunday night&rsquo;s game between Tampa Bay and Chicago because of an undisclosed health issue. Tirico put out a statement through NBC Sports PR early Sunday afternoon saying, &ldquo;I look forward to watching tonight,&rdquo; adding he would be back next Sunday. Noah Eagle filled in for Tirico. Football reporter Dianna Russini, who parted ways with The Athletic after allegations of an inappropriate relationship with New England Patriots coach Mike Vrabel, might return to media soon with a podcast sponsored by a gambling company, according to Front Office Sports . However, Pro Football Talk&rsquo;s Mike Florio said , &ldquo;If there&rsquo;s going to be actual reporting as part of this, you&rsquo;ve gotta have some credibility, or nobody&rsquo;s going to believe what you say. Is there going to be an effort to be an insider again? Unless and until we get an explanation that restores credibility&hellip; as somebody who aggregates the reports of others, amplifies the reports of others, wants my audience to believe the reporting of others, I&rsquo;ve gotta believe it first. And after everything that&rsquo;s happened, everything we know, I&rsquo;m not going to be inclined to believe anything. Which means I&rsquo;m not going to be amplifying anything. I don&rsquo;t care what the reporting is. Unless and until it&rsquo;s confirmed by someone who doesn&rsquo;t have these credibility issues, I&rsquo;m going to ignore it. Unless the credibility is somehow restored.&rdquo; Awful Announcing&rsquo;s Brendon Kleen has more . Hot type Phil Collins will be inducted into the Rock & Roll Hall of Fame later this month as a solo act. Here&rsquo;s The New York Times with &ldquo;Phil Collins Knew He Was &lsquo;Lucky,&rsquo; if Not Cool.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Build your influence and lead projects through Lead with Influence , a six-week online program designed for professionals without formal management experience. Learn to communicate effectively, manage up, give and receive feedback and gain buy-in across teams. Apply by Jan. 8, 2027. Protect your sources, your audience and yourself with specialized training on trauma, suicide, addiction and mental health disparities with The Mental Health Reporting Project   &mdash; developed in partnership with The Carter Center. Free due to the generous support of the Brain & Behavior Research Foundation. Funders: Learn how ",
