      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Paramount CEO David Ellison Can't Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family",
      "pageDescription": "Sen. Cory Booker, Democrat of New Jersey, is holding a &quot;spotlight hearing&quot; in Washington about Paramount Skydance&#039;s proposed $111 billion deal to acquire Warner Bros. Discovery. David Ellison, Paramount&#039;s chairman and CEO, will not be able to attend the hearing because of a death in the family, according to the company.",
      "evidenceText": "Paramount CEO David Ellison Can't Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family Paramount CEO David Ellison Can't Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family &times; Apr 15, 2026 5:51am PT Paramount CEO David Ellison Can’t Attend Senate Hearing on Warner Bros. Merger Because of Death in the Family, Company Says By Todd Spangler Plus Icon Todd Spangler NY Digital Editor xpangler Latest Adobe Is Adding AI Agent Features Across Its Product Lines, but Execs Emphasize Creators Have Choice of How They Use AI 2 hours ago Disney Taps Adobe as ‘Official AI Innovation Partner’ Under Multiyear Pact 21 hours ago Gwyneth Paltrow’s ‘Goop Guide’ Show Coming to Netflix and Spotify, in Which She Explains ‘How to Live Goop’ in ‘All the Ways That Can Be Defined’ 23 hours ago See All Add us as a Google Preferred Source Alberto E. Rodriguez/Getty Images for CinemaCon Sen. Cory Booker, Democrat of New Jersey, is holding a “spotlight hearing” Wednesday in Washington about Paramount Skydance ’s proposed $111 billion deal to acquire Warner Bros. Discovery . David Ellison , Paramount’s chairman and CEO, will not be able to attend the hearing because of a death in the family, according to the company. “Regretfully, Mr. Ellison is unable to be in Washington D.C. on Wednesday, as he is attending a funeral due to a death in the family,” Ted Lehman, Paramount Skydance’s SVP and head U.S. public policy and government affairs, wrote in a letter to Booker dated April 14. Booker had invited Ellison to speak at the hearing in a letter sent Monday. Related Stories Raye Named Variety’s 2026 Hitmaker of the Year Raye to Headline BST Hyde Park With Sienna Spiro Opening A Paramount Skydance rep declined to identify which member of the Ellison family had died. According to Lehman, Paramount’s megadeal to merge with WBD “is quite straightforward: We believe the transaction should be reviewed on the merits. And on the merits, the transaction is procompetitive.” Lehman continued, “Most importantly, a Paramount Skydance/Warner Bros. Discovery transaction is all about increasing the amount of content released in theaters and streaming platforms. This increase in content will create competition and greater demand for creative talent. We want customers in theaters, where they can experience the full moviegoing experience, and the increased number of theatrical releases that the combined company will generate will provide our streaming platform with an impressive array of content that will check the dominance of rivals like Netflix.” Ellison had previously declined an invitation to testify before the Senate Judiciary Committee’s Subcommittee on Antitrust, Competition Policy and Consumer Rights. The subcommittee held a hearing Feb. 3 where Netflix co-CEO Ted Sarandos  and Warner Bros. Discovery chief revenue and strategy officer Bruce Campbell testified amid sharp questioning. Following the hearing, Booker requested Ellison’s written testimony, “seeking insights on the proposed sale of Warner Bros. Discovery and Paramount’s parallel bid, and asking Ellison to address concerns about political interference in the merger review process,” according to a group of eight Democratic senators, including Booker. However, they said, “Ellison’s response failed to address these issues.” Those included questions about whether Ellison had ever donated money to Trump directly or indirectly and whether there had been any communications regarding a personal benefit to Trump with a potential Paramount-WBD merger. The senators also said the Feb. 19 letter served “as notice to preserve records related to the proposed Paramount-Warner Bros. Discovery transaction.” According to Booker’s letter this week, Paramount-Skydance has not responded to preservation letter signed by the eight U.S. senators. “As the leader of the company seeking to execute one of the largest media mergers in American history, your continued unwillingness to engage with Congressional oversight is itself a matter of public concern,” Booker wrote. Paramount has said the Warner Bros. Discovery merger would ensure creatives have “more avenues for their work, not fewer” and that the combined company would “greenlight more projects” and “back bold ideas.” Per Booker’s April 13 letter, “These are serious commitments. This forum is an opportunity for you to make them directly to Congress and to the workers, journalists, and creators whose livelihoods depend on whether those promises are kept.” Booker’s letter to Ellison also cited an open letter now signed by more than 2,000 Hollywood professionals as of Tuesday opposing the Paramount-WBD deal, saying in part that “The integrity, independence, and diversity of our industry would be grievously compromised” as a result of the merger. Signatories to the letter, available at BlocktheMerger.com , include Kristen Stewart, Ben Stiller, Florence Pugh, Pedro Pascal, Edward Norton, Atsuko Okatsuka and Joaquin Phoenix. The Senate hearing is slated for Wednesday starting at at 3 p.m. ET. Speakers scheduled to appear are: documentary filmmaker David Borenstein; Michael Isaac, director of legal services at the Writers Guild of America East; Katie Phang, attorney, legal analyst and former host of “The Katie Phang Show”; and Mara Verheyden-Hilliard, co-founder and executive director of the Partnership for Civil Justice Fund and member of the steering committee of Jane Fonda’s Committee for the First Amendment. In addition, actor Mark Ruffalo — who has strongly opposed the Paramount-WBD merger — will testify via videoconference at the hearing, according to Booker’s office. Paramount’s proposed Warner Bros. takeover is subject to approval by WBD shareholders at an April 23 special meeting. It also is pending regulatory approval. Last week, Paramount confirmed it brought on the  sovereign wealth funds of Saudi Arabia, Qatar and Abu Dhabi,  as well as LionTree Investment Fund, as equity investors in connection with the WBD takeover bid. In aggregate, the  three Middle Eastern funds are investing close to $24 billion , with Saudi Arabia’s Public Investment Fund taking a roughly $10 billion stake. Jump to Comments ‘De Gaulle’ Wins Top Prize at American French Film Festival as 30th Anniversary Edition Draws 18,000 Attendees Fantastic Fest Award Winner ‘Bloody Tennis,’ Starring Sandra Guldberg Kampp and Helena Zengel, Sells to Further Territories (EXCLUSIVE) Bong Joon Ho on Restoring ‘The Host’ and Why It Still Resonates 20 Years On: ‘We Are Repeating Our Mistakes’ ‘Supergirl’ Star Milly Alcock Sets Stage Play ‘Julie’ at London’s Old Vic Theater Lionsgate Acquires U.S. and U.K. Rights to Robert Lantos’ Epic Period Drama ‘Rise of the Raven’ (EXCLUSIVE) ‘Ramayana’ Makes History as First Indian Film to Top U.K., Ireland Box Office as ‘Godzilla Minus Zero’ Debuts in Third Loading comments... Most Popular Paul McCartney Becomes a Billionaire, Making Him the World’s Third-Richest Musician ‘Ramayana’ Makes History as First Indian Film to Top U.K., Ireland Box Office as ‘Godzilla Minus Zero’ Debuts in Third ‘Ebenezer’ First Reactions: Johnny Depp’s Hollywood Comeback Is a ‘Wickedly Fun Holiday Treat’ That Adds 'Refreshing New Layers' to the Christmas&hellip; DC Shocker: James Gunn, Peter Safran Out as Co-Chairmen, Marvel Veteran Jeremy Latcham to Lead Comic Book Studio Maya Jama Strikes Deal With Netflix (EXCLUSIVE) 'Artificial' Star Andrew Garfield Recalls Believing OpenAI's Sam Altman Was 'Genuinely Great' and Could Be 'My Future Best Friend' David Walliams, Former ‘Britain’s Got Talent’ Judge, Accused of ‘Grooming Fans’ as Young as 15 After Failing to Get&hellip; ‘Godzilla Minus Zero’ and ‘Ramayana: Rise of a Legend’ Roar at International Box Office DC in Flux: What Happens to James Gunn's Batman, Superman and Other Heroes After Shocking Exit? Julia Garner Says Madonna Biopic Status Changes 'Every Hour,' But She'd 'Love for the Movie to Get Made' Must Read Film Sex, Love and War: How the Racy Gay Epic 'La Bola Negra' Could Win Netflix's First Best Picture Oscar By Andrew Wallenstein 4 weeks Film Ben Affleck Explains It All: Artists Equity, the Death of Movie Stars and the Dilemma With Comic Book Movies Film Pete Davidson Grows Up: On Leaving 'SNL,' Famous Exes, the Joys of Fatherhood and Why He's Keeping His Hillary Clinton Tattoo Music ‘It’s Time for My Voice’: Jennifer Hudson on Reviving ‘Dreamgirls,’ Booking Tom Cruise for Her Talk Show and Writing Original Music&hellip; Film The New Chris Rock: The Comedy Icon on Directing ‘Misty Green’ and Not Returning to the Oscars Unless He’s Nominated Variety Close the menu Expand the sub menu Film News Reviews Podcasts Box Office Events Columns Trailers Expand the sub menu TV News Reviews Podcasts Ratings Columns Trailers Expand the sub menu What To Watch Lists & Features Expand the sub menu Music News Album Reviews Concert Reviews Hitmakers Music For",
