      "evidenceText": "60 Minutes Transcript: Donald Trump, the 2026 post-midterm elections interview - CBS News Watch CBS News This past Tuesday, President Donald Trump's Republican Party suffered an extraordinary midterm repudiation &ndash; losing not just the House and Senate, but statewide offices in red states, from Texas to Kansas to South Carolina, that Democrats hadn't won in decades. In the past, this president has met defeat with claims of fraud and the effort to overturn the 2020 election. But when we met with him in the White House on Friday for his first sit-down interview since the midterms, we found him in a surprisingly reflective mood, and seemingly accepting of defeat. Our wide-ranging conversation covered: immigration and corruption, Iran and artificial intelligence, his potential successors and his own mortality. But we began with the election results. Ross Douthat: Two years ago, you won a big, some might say historic, victory. Just now, the Republican party in the midterms suffered a big, some might say, historic defeat. Give me your political analysis of what just happened. President Donald Trump: Well, number one, I was not on the ballot. But I campaigned hard for some people. And-- I think if I was on the ballot, it would've been a much different situation because you saw the level of popularity and love. There was love. We had massive rallies. You had to transfer those voters, however, to candidates that they didn't like. But there's something beyond that. They didn't like the war. They didn't like the fact that gasoline went up, 'cause I had it down at a low, and there's-- a great dislike, incredibly, for Israel.  Ross Douthat: Let's go through those then one by one. President Donald Trump: Go ahead. Ross Douthat: Start-- start with the candidates, right? You said it was tough to transfer your own appeal to candidates President Donald Trump: Yeah. Ross Douthat: But in a lot of cases, you picked the candidates. You endorsed Ken Paxton-- President Donald Trump: Well, I-- Ross Douthat: --in Texas. President Donald Trump: I-- Ross Douthat: He had a tough time. President Donald Trump: Sure. Ross Douthat: You endorsed Darline Graham-- President Donald Trump: But-- but Cornyn-- Ross Douthat: --in South Carolina. President Donald Trump: --Cornyn would've lost also. Darline, I did that in-- honor of a man named Lindsey, who was a friend of mine. And she fought hard. She fought hard. She really did.  Ross Douthat: She lost a very Republican state. President Donald Trump: Oh-- Ross Douthat: To a very liberal challenger-- President Donald Trump: I won-- I won South Carolina by the highest vote in history by far. I won Texas by the highest vote in history by far. I won Florida who, by the way, I did endorse. And he's gonna be-- a great governor and a great senator. I endorsed both of them. And, had I not endorsed them, they wouldn't have won. Ross Douthat: Why can't it transfer? You-- you were out on the trail, and you said-- President Donald Trump: It didn't transfer-- Ross Douthat: You said, \"Vote like I'm on the ballot.\" President Donald Trump: Yeah. It didn't-- Ross Douthat: And people didn't? Or-- or did they? Did they vote because they were mad at you-- President Donald Trump: No, they-- Ross Douthat: About-- President Donald Trump: I think they didn't vote, really. I think in some cases, they didn't like the candidates. And that's, you know, their prerogative.  Ross Douthat: Couple days ago your vice president, JD Vance, wrote a social media post where he says, \"The reason we lost is the war,\" meaning Iran. And he said things in that post-- President Donald Trump: I don't disagree-- Ross Douthat: About Israel. Yeah. So you agree with his analysis? President Donald Trump: I don't disagree with it  Ross Douthat: Did you know he was gonna write that post? President Donald Trump: He asked me what do I think. I said, \"You write what you're--\" I said-- Ross Douthat: \"Write what you--\" President Donald Trump: You write what you want--\" Ross Douthat: \"Write what you feel\"? President Donald Trump: By the way, \"What are you gonna write? \"The only thing I wish he would've said there, if he felt it, and you'd have to ask him about that, but if you had to do it again, would you do it? You know, he's part of a decision-making process. And my answer is an emphatic yes because you can't let Iran have a nuclear weapon. I think, historically, this will be a very good decision. Is it a good decision for today and tomorrow? Maybe not. But you know, I have to go with what's right for our country. If they had a nuclear weapon during my term, I would be excoriated far worse than what's happened. So-- Ross Douthat: All right. Let's-- let's-- President Donald Trump: I've stopped-- Ross Douthat: Come-- President Donald Trump: But Ross, I've stopped them from having a nuclear weapon. That's a big thing. The president argued that even with the Iran war, the U.S. economy in his second term has been very strong. Ross Douthat: But in poll after poll, people say they're unhappy with this economy in a way they weren't in the first term. Cost of living, inflation, and so on. Is that all just the Iran war? President Donald Trump: Well, it's not really the Iran war because the numbers are phenomenal. I think it was bad messaging, maybe by me, but I would talk about it all the time. So I don't know, how much can I talk about it? Look, the press doesn't cover-- positive news for Republicans. They don't cover certainly positive news for me. So I have to do it myself. I have to get it out there. We have historically good numbers. We have the highest stock market in history. Ross Douthat: But why don't Americans feel it, that-- President Donald Trump: They don't-- Ross Douthat: Why-- why don't they-- 'cause they're-- President Donald Trump: Because I think-- Ross Douthat: Obviously, like, you and the press, you and the media, you and our business have been in tension throughout your career. President Donald Trump: Correct. Ross Douthat: But you still won elections, right? President Donald Trump: Yeah. Ross Douthat: So why couldn't you win this time-- President Donald Trump: I think it was bad messaging. Ross Douthat: What was the bad message? President Donald Trump: We couldn't get the word across.  Whether it was the message or the record, the Republican collapse will give Democrats in Congress new powers over budgets, hearings, and subpoenas. Ross Douthat: You came out after the midterms. You congratulated the Democrats. President Donald Trump: Correct. Ross Douthat: You said that you were open to working with them. President Donald Trump: True. Ross Douthat: What is one thing you would like to work with a Democratic majority on-- President Donald Trump: I'd love to do something on immigration. Ross Douthat: What? President Donald Trump: Because-- well, so Ross Douthat: Do you think immigration hurt you in the midterms?  President Donald Trump: I think-- Ross Douthat: Do you think the crackdown hurt you-- President Donald Trump: I think it both helped and hurt. I think I got an A+ for closing up the border. I get very high marks for getting the bad ones out. But I get hurt because we also took people that had good jobs, that-- that created one violation, or breaking of the law, and that was that they came into the country illegally. Now, there is a large part of the country that think that they should be put out. And there's a large part of the country that think that they should be allowed to stay if they're good people working in a landscaping company, or workin' in a hotel, or doing something, right? And in some cases, paying taxes. And there's a large group of people who think that they should be allowed to stay.  Ross Douthat: So you think your deportation policy-- President Donald Trump: I think it was-- Ross Douthat: Went too far-- President Donald Trump: Too tough. Ross Douthat: It was too tough? President Donald Trump: I think it might be too tough, yeah. Ross Douthat: What would-- President Donald Trump: And-- and I think-- Ross Douthat: What does a deal look like President Donald Trump: I think ultimately-- Ross Douthat: With the Democrats? President Donald Trump: That hurt me. Well, the deal would be we would work something out. And I think it's something-- I think that's something that's actually doable. Ross Douthat: Doable to create-- President Donald Trump: When you go into-- Ross Douthat: Some kinda-- some kind of legal status-- President Donald Trump: Yeah, Ross, when you go in-- Ross Douthat: For the landscapers and the-- President Donald Trump: Yeah. When you go into a hotel and you have a woman that's worked there as-- you know, as a maid, as a cleaner, as a this, as a that, as a waitress, a lotta good things, great jobs, and all of a sudden, they're being taken outta the country, it's tough. It's a tough thing. But there's a group of people that I would say on the sort of pretty far right that would like everybody taken out. Ross Douthat: Do some of them-- President Donald Trump: Then there's a group of people on the-- Ross Douthat: Do-- do any of those people work in your administration? Are they in-- President Donald Trump: Yes. Ross Douthat: The building with us right now? President Donald Trump: Yeah, no, no. Well, there're a lotta those people. Then there's also people that are more modified on that. In other words, get the killers out. Get all the-- the rough ones out. Get the drug dealers out. But let the people that are here, and in some cases here for ten and 15 years, work a deal for them. Ross Douthat: You think the Democratic party right now has people in it who would be interested in this kind of deal? President Donald Trump: I think so, yeah. People have already called me. Ross Douthat: So I'll say I saw James Talarico, the new Democratic senator-- President Donald Trump: Said very nice things, yeah-- Ross Douthat: From Texas, I saw him on Fox News. And he said immigration was one of the things he wanted-- President Donald Trump: Others-- Ross Douthat: To work with you on-- President Donald Trump: Others too. South Carolina said. Numerous-- Ross Douthat: You did-- you did-- President Donald Trump: Numerous people that won-- Ross Douthat: In the course-- President Donald Trump: Said that. Ross Douthat: Of the campaign, you did occasionally call him Talafreako. President Donald Trump: Yeah, I did. Ross Douthat: Do you think you could work with the Democrats without insulting them-- President Donald Trump: Yeah, I do-- Ross Douthat: Consistently? President Donald Trump: I could. Ross Douthat: You think so-- President Donald Trump: I also call-- I also-- Ross Douthat: But you-- President Donald Trump: Call many of them Dumocrats  Ross Douthat: You did that just-- President Donald Trump: 'Cause &mdash; Ross Douthat: The other day. If-- if it took six months of not insulting the Democrats to get a deal done, do you think you could do it? President Donald Trump: Very easily. Ross Douthat: Okay. That'll be interesting&ndash; President Donald Trump: But you have to understand-- Ross Douthat: To watch. Do you think you could-- President Donald Trump: I'm here-- I'm here-- Ross Douthat: Do it if they're-- President Donald Trump: To help the country. Ross Douthat: Do you think-- think you could do it if they're investigating you? If they're running investigations into your administration, can you work with them? President Donald Trump: Well, I did it for four years. You know, I-- they-- what they did with me was-- there's never been weaponization. They impeached me twice. And there are those that say that worked out to my advantage. I mean, you know, 'cause Ross Douthat: Do you think they'll impeach you again? President Donald Trump: I can't imagine it, you know? They d-- hey, look, what have I done? I've had the best two years in the history of the presidency.  All this winning, according to the president, doesn't preclude some change of White House personnel. Ross Douthat: In a previous lifetime, you were rather famous for saying, \"You're fired\" quite often.  President Donald Trump: Yeah Ross Douthat: After a big midterm defeat like this, do you expect to fire anyone from your team&ndash; President Donald Trump: I do. Yeah. Ross Douthat: You do? President Donald Trump: Little bit. Ross Douthat: Little bit? President Donald Trump: I love my Cabinet. I think they're good. But I'll do-- be doin' maybe a few changes.  The first change came an hour after our conversation with the president. Susie Wiles, the president's chief of staff, was replaced by Deputy Chief of Staff Stephen Miller, the architect of the president's mass deportation policy. It's a move that seems to conflict with the president's professed desire to work with Democrats on immigration. We asked the president about another administration official whose job security is the subject of speculation. Ross Douthat: We saw Secretary of Defense Pete Hegseth in the hall. He was meeting with you just before now. His name has come up a few times-- President Donald Trump: It has. Ross Douthat: As someone people have called for his resignation. President Donald Trump: They do. Ross Douthat: Do you have confidence in him? President Donald Trump: I do. Look, we've won two wars. We've won Venezuela and taken out billions and billions and billions of dollars already. And we will be taking out many billions of dollars and get along very well. And-- you could call it a territory, you could call it whatever you want. We won that war in less than one hour. Ross Douthat: And you credit Hegseth-- President Donald Trump: And nobody was killed. Ross Douthat: You credit Pete Hegseth for that-- President Donald Trump: No. But he was a part of it.  This past week, Hegseth released a long video message defending his own tenure and the prosecution of the Iran war &ndash; a war the president claims is essentially finished. Ross Douthat: So you're not gonna escalate the war now that the midterms are over President Donald Trump: Well, I'm not looking to. I don't think there's any reason to. I don't think there's any reason to. Look--  Ross Douthat: They still have nuclear material-- President Donald Trump: They have-- Ross Douthat: Right? President Donald Trump: They have nothing. It's buried under thousands of tons of rock.  Ross Douthat: So what's the deal in the end with them-- President Donald Trump: The deal-- Ross Douthat: That gets-- President Donald Trump: The-- Ross Douthat: That gets the Strait open-- President Donald Trump: The deal-- Ross Douthat: 'Cause it's important-- it's important to stress-- President Donald Trump: No, no, the d-- Ross Douthat: 'Cause-- President Donald Trump: The strait is open. We have ships-- Ross Douthat: But-- President Donald Trump: Going-- Ross Douthat: But the ships-- President Donald Trump: Through-- Ross Douthat: That are going through are being babysat by a large U.S. military presence. So how do we make a deal-- President Donald Trump: Not a very-- Ross Douthat: That gets that-- President Donald Trump: Not very expensive-- Ross Douthat: Presence out of there? President Donald Trump: Not very expensive. Ross Douthat: We can just leave-- President Donald Trump: And-- Ross Douthat: Them there-- President Donald Trump: At the end of-- Ross Douthat: Indefinitely? President Donald Trump: This whole thing, those countries that we've protected are going to pay us back any and all costs that we've had for the war 'cause without us, they wouldn't exist. Without us, Israel would not exist. Let me tell you Ross Douthat: Israel &mdash; President Donald Trump: Israel-- Ross Douthat: Tell me-- tell me about-- President Donald Trump: Israel would not exist except for Donald Trump because we went in with those beautiful B2 Bombers more than a year ago, and we hit the three sites at-- at the time. Ross Douthat: But on Israel, do you have regrets about the decision to help the Israelis do decapitation strikes on Iran because that changed the Iranian regime, and it seems like it made them escalate. You could've just kept hitting the-- President Donald Trump: Well-- Ross Douthat: The nuclear facilities, right-- President Donald Trump: Yeah, you could've done that, but-- we had people that w-- would have gone further. We decapitated their nuclear arsenal. Look, I deal with these people. I deal with a lotta people, and that's what I've done my whole life. I make deals. And obviously, I make good deals 'cause I beca-- I made a lotta money and I became president. But-- you know, it's-- that's a deal too. One place where the president's dealmaking has thus far fallen short is the war between Russia and Ukraine. Our interview came just days before Ukrainian, Russian, and American delegations were scheduled to meet in Abu Dhabi for the first time since February. Ross Douthat: You came into office promising to make peace in Ukraine. President Donald Trump: Yeah. Ross Douthat: There is still a war going on. What's the biggest obstacle to peace? President Donald Trump: It was the hatred between Zelenskyy and Putin. If I had been president that war would've never started. Zero chance. But it did start. So I inherited that war. I believe we're very close to making a deal-- Ross Douthat: Who-- who do you trust more, President Putin or President Zelenskyy-- President Donald Trump: Well, I'd rather not say, but I get along with both. I believe that that war is going to end, and it's gonna end soon. And the only reason it's gonna end is because of me. There is tremendous hatred between President Putin and President Zelenskyy. And-- you know, I-- Ross Douthat: Isn't it more understandable coming from President Zelenskyy, given that his country was actually invaded? President Donald Trump: Well, it is. But when they had their first meeting there was great disrespect shown to Russia. So I'm not gonna say anything-- Ross Douthat: I mean, but balancing out-- President Donald Trump: Well-- well-- Ross Douthat: Invading, disrespecting-- President Donald Trump: Let's-- let's put it this way: the war never happened during my four years, and it was never going to happen, even though it was the apple of President Putin's eye. Ross Douthat: Do you think he is stringing you along? President Donald Trump: Oh, I don't think so. Ross Douthat: 'Cause you've been saying over and over you're gonna make peace. And it just seems like he's the obstacle in the end-- President Donald Trump: Well, there's been nobody that's been tougher on Russia than me. Look at our trade. Look what I've done to them on trade with the sanctions and everything else.  Ross Douthat: You've gone back and forth. You-- at some points you have done lots of things, big things in support of Ukraine. But just recently you lifted diesel sanctions on Russia. Like, you've done various things to take pressure off Russia-- President Donald Trump: I didn't lift diesel sanctions. I made it possible for Russia to give not us, but to the world-- thousands and thousands of barrels of oil, of diesel, which it needed. Didn't-- do any big favor.  Ross Douthat: But you expect it to all work out with a free and independent Ukraine-- President Donald Trump: I think we're gonna have it done, and I think it's gonna happen and they're gonna end up with a little bit different shape Ukraine.  President Donald Trump has been the dominant figure in American politics for a decade. But now his time is running out. In the next two years, Mr. Trump will need to decide whether to endorse a successor. He'll have to deal with accusations of corruption, a major concern for midterm voters, over wealth he piled up during his time in office. He will make consequential decisions about artificial intelligence, amid fears that AI threatens the future of the human race. And all of this, in the shadow of the 80-year-old president's own mortality. Ross Douthat: In two years someone else is gonna be the Republican nominee for president. President Donald Trump: Correct. Ross Dou",
