      "pageDescription": "If you",
      "evidenceText": "Rioting for a reason | The European Correspondent The European Correspondent Support the crowdfunding Projects About us Rioting for a reason If you've glanced at the news lately, you've probably seen many reports about riots, looting, and violence at protests across Europe. These protests are driven mostly by young people demanding better school conditions and affordable housing, among other things. Yet many headlines focus on violence or riots rather than protestors' demands. Of the more than 10,000 news reports of protests in France by European outlets, just 8% of headlines explicitly mentioned protest demands. Meanwhile, around one in three headlines mentioned terms related to violence. In fact, some of the most-used words in headlines are associated with violence, including “riot”, “clash”, “chaos”, and “violence” itself. Sebastian Gräff 4 November 2026 at 02:00 All news reports were gathered by the GDELT project between 17 September and 1 November. We analysed news reports from European countries about France, where mentions of protests, strikes, or violent unrest occurred within the first 300 characters. We verified protests using an LLM (Gemini) and used it to classify whether news headlines included references to violence or protestors’ demands. Want Europe in your pocket? The best of our journalism. Made to fit your pocket or purse. Once a month in your letterbox. 424 0 PocketPaper subscriptions pre-ordered so far Pre-orders open until 30 November 2026 To launch the PocketPaper, we need 3,000 subscribers. Will you be one of them? 12 editions for €120 a year. You'll only be charged for the PocketPaper if we reach our goal. Pre-order now Europe, visualised Understand how Europe is changing in graphs, charts, and maps. Incumbents lose elections Ever dreamt of living in a castle? Trains are still safe Rioting for a reason European Union What's up in Brussels? Frontex is eyeing sweeping new powers I See You, ICC EU governments are sweating over trade with China Spare some change for the EU? Our best stories Our best, timeless journalism The MEP's guide to milking the European Parliament MEPs face little chance of punishment if they want to squeeze as much as they can from a generous system. Want to understand Europe? Hit the road For the past six decades, millions of Europeans drive down South together around this time of year. Missing the way we never were How the Netherlands led the dance revolution A tale of two footprints",
